WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:02.710
For most illnesses,

00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:05.580
a vaccine is an almost
guaranteed lifeline.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:07.470
But influenza is different.

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:09.430
We have a very good measles vaccine.

00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:11.460
We have a very good rubella vaccine,

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:13.770
very good mumps vaccine.

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:18.260
All of these viruses in the
population haven't changed

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:21.410
over the last 50, 60, 70 years.

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:25.209
In contrast, influenza changes every year.

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:27.790
The World Health Organization

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:32.220
estimates that influenza infects
1 billion of us every year;

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:35.383
causing up to 650,000 deaths worldwide.

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:38.570
Vaccines are critical,

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:41.060
but they're only around 50% effective

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:43.310
because they don't fight
every strain of flu.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:48.300
So scientists around the
world are searching for a shot

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.560
that fights different
strains of the virus

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:53.380
including some that
might not even exist yet.

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:54.540
It's really very exciting,

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.840
because you know that you're
working for something good

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:59.260
that may change the whole world

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:01.410
and the health of many people.

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:03.600
This breakthrough will
give us lasting protection

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.910
against influenza, but it
might also help us understand

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:08.923
how we get there with other viruses too.

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:18.400
Difficulty breathing, highly contagious,

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:19.913
returning year after year.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:24.840
Hippocrates first described
influenza in 412 BC.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:28.120
Since then, the virus has
caused annual epidemics.

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:30.800
Most notably the Spanish flu in 1918

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:34.680
that killed an estimated 50 to
100 million people worldwide

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:36.050
and changed the world in ways

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:37.893
that seem all too familiar today.

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:44.580
1918, when the influenza H1N1 showed up,

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:47.790
was during a period
of a lot of problems

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:49.220
in the United States at the time.

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:52.310
World War I was ongoing at that time,

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:56.900
there was a lot of movement
from rural areas into cities.

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:59.750
So at the time we didn't
even know it was a virus.

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:02.240
Viruses were kinda still
being figured out at the time.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:04.110
So it really was not until the 1930s,

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:07.330
that we knew the cause
of the 1918 pandemic

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:10.530
and then subsequently
the seasonal epidemics

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.700
that we would have every winter

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:15.860
that they realized that
was actually due to a virus.

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:17.630
And so from the '30s,

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:22.300
it wasn't really until the
late 1940s and 1950s

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.900
that actual vaccines were
being used more regularly.

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:28.490
Even though there are just
three basic types of flu,

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:32.240
A, B and C; type A which is
the most dangerous for humans,

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:36.490
has a surface covered in
two main proteins, H and N.

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:38.720
There are 18 different types of H protein

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:40.910
and 11 types of N protein.

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:45.290
And they combine to create
up to 198 influenza subtypes

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:49.010
including ones like H3N2 and H1N1.

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:51.890
The influenza virus is
constantly changing.

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:54.630
And so if you look at a virus,

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:57.170
on the inside of the virus are the genes,

00:02:57.170 --> 00:02:58.830
and genes are just the recipe,

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:01.870
the instructions for making
more copies of itself.

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:05.170
So if somebody sneezes or coughs,

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:06.850
and you happen to breathe that in,

00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:09.300
those viruses that are in
those respiratory droplets

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:10.940
go to the back of your throat

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:15.210
they grab on to the respiratory
cells that are there.

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:17.620
They push themselves into the cells

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:21.060
and then release those
genes that are in there.

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:24.940
Those genes, it's not one
long string of instructions,

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:28.500
it's eight separate
strings of instruction.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:33.500
And so there's so much opportunity
for that virus to mess up

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:36.530
in terms of making copies of itself.

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:38.850
It's not a very good editor.

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:42.910
It really doesn't have
good quality control.

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:45.200
In one way, that's bad for the virus

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:48.860
beause it makes copies of itself
that may not even live on,

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:50.780
they don't even reproduce.

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:54.080
But it also means that it can find ways

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:56.900
to get around your own immunity,

00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:59.787
and ways to get around
vaccines that are being used.

00:03:59.787 --> 00:04:02.473
to get around antiviral drugs as well.

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:05.040
This genetic shape-shifting,

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:06.920
is known as antigenic drift.

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.700
And means that our body can't
always recognize the virus,

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:13.030
even if we've caught it
or been vaccinated before.

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:16.000
But scientists at pharmaceutical
company BiondVax,

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.950
have worked for almost three
decades creating a vaccine

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:21.710
which targets parts of the
virus that don't change.

00:04:21.710 --> 00:04:24.460
All influenza virus are
different in their appearance

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:26.580
but they are still considered as influenza

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:28.780
because they have a lot in common.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:31.447
And what the immune system wants to do it,

00:04:31.447 --> 00:04:35.384
they want to recognize
the virus and to attack it

00:04:35.384 --> 00:04:37.324
when it enters the body.

00:04:37.324 --> 00:04:38.910
So we have to teach the immune system

00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:40.180
how the virus looks like.

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:42.460
So what we thought will be more effective

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:45.250
and to have a universal vaccine,

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:48.500
is to show the immune system and teach it

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:52.190
to recognize the conserved
and common denominator

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:54.570
of all influenza viruses.

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:58.830
So we constructed a
vaccine, it is called M-001.

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:02.470
It contains nine such conserved regions

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:03.720
from the influenza virus.

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:07.940
Variations between influenza viruses,

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:10.230
usually happen on the surface.

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:11.360
But dig a little deeper,

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:14.110
and there are similarities
in its peptides;

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:15.890
short strings of amino acids

00:05:15.890 --> 00:05:19.000
that are responsible for
how the virus functions.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:22.250
M-001 is made up of
nine different peptides

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:24.600
that our immune system
can learn to recognize

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:26.000
and fight off in the future.

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:29.970
One of the peptides that
we selected in our vaccine

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:33.270
is very close to the point where the virus

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:34.910
is entering the cells.

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:36.376
So it's like a key and the lock.

00:05:36.376 --> 00:05:38.540
Where the key is on the side of the virus,

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:41.013
and the lock is the entrance to our cells.

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:44.550
And if the virus wants to enter the cell

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:48.410
and cause the disease, it
enter its key into the lock,

00:05:48.410 --> 00:05:52.750
opens it, and the
disease starts to develop.

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:57.670
So by our vaccine, we block this key

00:05:57.670 --> 00:06:01.390
and the virus cannot
anymore enter into the cell

00:06:01.390 --> 00:06:02.880
and we prevent the disease.

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:05.940
So M-001 is in phase
three clinical trials.

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:07.550
Are you feeling nervous about the results?

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:09.160
Of course, I'm worried all the time.

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:12.250
I hardly breathe

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:13.810
until we get the outcomes

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:16.180
of our pivotal phase three trial

00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:19.300
which is a trial was
over 12,000 participants

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:21.200
that was conducted in Europe.

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:24.880
In this trial, we injected
half of the participants

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:26.460
with our vaccine,

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:29.650
and the other half received placebo, which was a saline.

00:06:29.650 --> 00:06:30.920
And at the end of the trial,

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:32.870
which means at the end of this month,

00:06:32.870 --> 00:06:34.820
we'll know how many participants

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:37.160
were ill in the experimental group

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:40.600
that was immunized with our
vaccine, and how many were ill

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:44.150
in the control group that
actually was not immunized.

00:06:44.150 --> 00:06:48.200
And if the difference
would be high enough,

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:51.260
then our vaccine works
and then we're happy.

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:53.150
We will know if it is safe,

00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:57.793
if it is protective against influenza.

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:01.060
A universal vaccine, like M-001,

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:04.470
would radically change the
way we make our flu shots.

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:06.750
Currently, for Northern
Hemisphere vaccines,

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:10.730
every February WHO picks
four strains of influenza

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:11.850
which scientists predict

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:14.520
will most likely be
circulating in October,

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:16.450
the start of the flu season.

00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:19.310
This vaccine recipe is
sent out to manufacturers

00:07:19.310 --> 00:07:20.610
and the majority of vaccines

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:22.530
are made just like back in the 40s,

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:25.940
by injecting the vaccine
virus into fertilized eggs.

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:27.310
It takes at least six months

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:29.840
to produce the high quantities we need.

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:31.570
So we have to really base

00:07:31.570 --> 00:07:36.170
on what happened two
months before February,

00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:39.940
but we are not really good
at that, unfortunately.

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:42.600
Dr. Peter Palese is a microbiologist,

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:45.270
who built the first genetic
map of the influenza virus

00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:47.100
back in the '70s.

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:49.010
The yearly influenza vaccine,

00:07:49.010 --> 00:07:52.150
I think is underappreciated,
underutilized.

00:07:52.150 --> 00:07:56.610
So I think there is a benefit
in taking the vaccine,

00:07:56.610 --> 00:08:00.050
because even if you get
the flu, it was a failure

00:08:00.050 --> 00:08:04.240
but your disease
morbidity is much lower.

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:07.820
Clearly, I think I want to
encourage your listeners

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:09.770
to take the current vaccine,

00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:11.600
the current influenza virus vaccine,

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:15.530
as an insurance against
whatever might happen, yeah.

00:08:15.530 --> 00:08:17.790
So it is a very safe vaccine.

00:08:17.790 --> 00:08:19.620
I think it is a very good vaccine.

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:23.030
Having said that, if we want
to make a better vaccine,

00:08:23.030 --> 00:08:24.860
we have to show that it is at least

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:27.620
as good as the current vaccine.

00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:30.220
But also that it really preempts

00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:34.860
or knows what the other
strains in year three,

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:36.800
four or five from now, will be.

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:40.950
And also the potential
new pandemic strains.

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:43.310
Dr. Palese's team is also part of the race

00:08:43.310 --> 00:08:45.163
to create a universal vaccine.

00:08:46.146 --> 00:08:48.650
Like BiondVax, they're
using an area of influenza

00:08:48.650 --> 00:08:50.080
that that doesn't mutate.

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:53.600
The stem of the H protein
on the surface of the virus.

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:56.210
We are redirecting the immune system

00:08:56.210 --> 00:08:58.170
towards these conserved regions.

00:08:58.170 --> 00:09:02.540
And I have to say, I
can protect any animal,

00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:04.570
whether it's a mouse,
whether it's a ferret,

00:09:04.570 --> 00:09:07.470
whether it's a guinea
pig against any strain.

00:09:07.470 --> 00:09:11.760
So using these constructs

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:14.990
which present the conserved
domain of the virus,

00:09:14.990 --> 00:09:17.620
it works beautifully in animals.

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:22.240
But remember, mice are not
man, ferrets are not humans.

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:27.240
I think we make progress,
but it is still not there.

00:09:27.730 --> 00:09:32.380
And I can't promise you
that it will be two years

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:35.220
or three years; we
cannot really predict

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:37.070
how long it will take

00:09:37.070 --> 00:09:41.084
that we will have such a
universal influenza virus vaccine.

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:45.690
With disappointing results

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:48.380
in the M-001 phase three trials,

00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:50.920
the wait will be longer
than some had hoped.

00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:52.890
But the research and
time that's been invested

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:55.334
isn't wasted, far from it.

00:09:55.667 --> 00:09:56.784
Could the work we're doing

00:09:56.784 --> 00:09:59.150
to find a universal flu
vaccine help in the way

00:09:59.150 --> 00:10:01.550
that we tackle viruses like Covid-19?

00:10:01.550 --> 00:10:03.010
Absolutely the other way around too.

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:07.540
And so the work that now
with significant resources

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:12.173
and speed being put into Covid right now,

00:10:13.370 --> 00:10:17.900
could have a significant
impact on flu vaccine choices.

00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:20.220
If you have a system in place

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:23.740
where you can make those
kinds of vaccines fast,

00:10:23.740 --> 00:10:26.760
as soon as you see an
emerging, a pandemic influenza,

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:29.010
you can start making vaccine immediately,

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:31.130
so faster vaccines.

00:10:31.130 --> 00:10:33.080
And so I think we'll
learn a lot about that

00:10:33.080 --> 00:10:34.560
with the Covid vaccines as well.

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:38.060
So all of that together,
we would love to see

00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:41.250
what we're learning with
Covid, be applied to flu.

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:43.070
Because what we're doing
right now with COVID

00:10:43.070 --> 00:10:45.590
is highly informed by what we were doing

00:10:45.590 --> 00:10:47.170
with flu previously.

00:10:47.170 --> 00:10:48.230
What would be the next steps

00:10:48.230 --> 00:10:49.710
if we got a universal vaccine?

00:10:49.710 --> 00:10:53.270
What would be wonderful,
is to get to that place

00:10:53.270 --> 00:10:55.500
where flu vaccines aren't something

00:10:55.500 --> 00:10:59.667
that are just for middle
and higher income countries.

00:10:59.667 --> 00:11:02.190
That low and middle
income countries globally

00:11:02.190 --> 00:11:05.200
have a significant
impact of flu every year.

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:11.010
But a program to vaccinate
children and adults every year

00:11:11.010 --> 00:11:13.520
in a country with billions of people,

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:17.250
is really complicated and
can be pretty expensive.

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:19.500
Coming up with a simple solution

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:23.030
that can be rolled out
in a programmatic way

00:11:23.030 --> 00:11:26.280
where it's cost effective
for that country to do it,

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:28.490
would have a significant impact

00:11:28.490 --> 00:11:30.333
for a better functioning country.

